CC.provideCourse.bind(null,"bridge")({"lessons":{"bridge-01":{"html":"<p>In spring 2027, four courses build directly on semester 1. <strong>Programming Basics II</strong> and <strong>Object Oriented Programming</strong> together make up 12 of the 30 ECTS credits in spring, so getting C and SQL solid first gives you the biggest head start.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Ask your department</div><p>Check with the department secretary whether you will take the fall courses next autumn, or whether any previous credits can transfer. Either way, knowing this material puts you on the same footing as your classmates.</p></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-spring-courses-and-what-they-need\"><h2 id=\"spring-courses-and-what-they-need\">Spring courses and what they need</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Spring course</th><th>ECTS</th><th>First spring topics</th><th>What it assumes from semester 1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Programming Basics II (CMP154)</strong></td><td>5</td><td>Advanced flowcharts, <code>struct</code>s (arrays of structs, nested structs), pointers, string functions, file I/O (add/list/update/delete records), testing</td><td>All of CMP153, especially <a href=\"#/lesson/c-11\">Functions</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-12\">Arrays</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-13\">Pointers</a> and <a href=\"#/lesson/c-15\">Strings</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Object Oriented Programming (CMP157)</strong></td><td>7</td><td>Java fundamentals, Eclipse IDE, classes &amp; objects, access modifiers, sorting &amp; searching, inheritance, polymorphism, UML</td><td><a href=\"#/lesson/c-05\">Variables</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-07\">Operators</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-08\">Decisions</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-09\">Loops</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/c-12\">Arrays</a> — Java syntax looks very similar to C</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Database Management Systems II (CMP152)</strong></td><td>7</td><td>Re-covers ER &amp; normalization quickly, then SQL functions, advanced queries, T-SQL transactions &amp; concurrency, tuning, security, backup</td><td>All of CMP151: <a href=\"#/lesson/db-03\">ER diagrams</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/db-04\">Normalization</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/db-09\">SELECT</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/db-11\">JOINs</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mathematics II (MTH154)</strong></td><td>4</td><td>Functions, logarithms, trigonometry, matrices, limits, derivatives, integrals</td><td><a href=\"#/lesson/math-06\">Exponents</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/math-07\">Radicals</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/math-08\">Factoring</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/math-09\">Equations</a>, <a href=\"#/lesson/math-11\">Inequalities</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Spring also includes <strong>Turkish Language II</strong>, <strong>Atatürk Principles II</strong> and <strong>Advanced English Skills II</strong>, which continue their semester 1 counterparts.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-recommended-priority-order\"><h2 id=\"recommended-priority-order\">Recommended priority order</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>C programming and SQL</strong> fully solid (do the mock exams and coding/SQL challenges).</li><li><strong>Bridge previews for C (<code>struct</code> and files) and Java</strong> (lessons 2–5 in this module).</li><li><strong>Database functions &amp; views preview</strong> (lesson 6) and <strong>Math II preview</strong> (lessons 7–8).</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>struct</strong></td><td>a custom C type that groups related variables under one name</td><td lang=\"tr\">yapı (struct)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>class</strong></td><td>a blueprint for objects in Java (data + methods together)</td><td lang=\"tr\">sınıf</td></tr><tr><td><strong>view</strong></td><td>a saved SQL SELECT query that acts like a virtual table</td><td lang=\"tr\">görünüm (view)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>logarithm</strong></td><td>the inverse operation of an exponent</td><td lang=\"tr\">logaritma</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>","id":"bridge-01","title":"What semester 2 expects from you","goal":"know which semester 1 topics each spring course needs and in what order to review them","week":"8","minutes":10,"terms":[{"t":"struct","m":"a custom C type that groups related variables under one name","tr":"yapı (struct)"},{"t":"class","m":"a blueprint for objects in Java (data + methods together)","tr":"sınıf"},{"t":"view","m":"a saved SQL SELECT query that acts like a virtual table","tr":"görünüm (view)"},{"t":"logarithm","m":"the inverse operation of an exponent","tr":"logaritma"}]},"bridge-02":{"html":"<p>In semester 1 you stored a student's name in one array and their grade in another. A <strong>struct</strong> (<em>structure</em>) lets you bundle different types of data for one thing — like a student's id, name and GPA — into a single custom type.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-defining-and-using-a-struct\"><h2 id=\"defining-and-using-a-struct\">Defining and using a struct</h2>\n<p>You declare the shape of the struct once, usually at the top of the file. Adding <code>typedef</code> lets you write <code>Student</code> instead of <code>struct Student</code> every time.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    int id;\n    char name[30];\n    double gpa;\n} Student;\n\nint main() {\n    Student s1 = {101, \"Deniz\", 3.45};\n    Student s2;\n    s2.id = 102;\n    strcpy(s2.name, \"Ece\");\n    s2.gpa = 3.80;\n\n    printf(\"%d: %s (GPA %.2f)\\n\", s1.id, s1.name, s1.gpa);\n    printf(\"%d: %s (GPA %.2f)\\n\", s2.id, s2.name, s2.gpa);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p>Use the dot operator <code>.</code> to read or change a field (<code>s1.gpa</code>). Notice that <code>s2.name</code> is a <code>char</code> array, so we copy text into it with <code>strcpy</code> rather than <code>=</code>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-arrays-of-structs-and-nested-structs\"><h2 id=\"arrays-of-structs-and-nested-structs\">Arrays of structs and nested structs</h2>\n<p>You can make an array of structs just like an array of <code>int</code>, and you can put one struct inside another (<strong>nested struct</strong>).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    int day;\n    int month;\n    int year;\n} Date;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    char name[20];\n    Date birth;\n    int grade;\n} Student;\n\nvoid printStudent(Student s) {\n    printf(\"%s | born %02d/%02d/%d | grade %d\\n\",\n           s.name, s.birth.day, s.birth.month, s.birth.year, s.grade);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Student cls[2] = {\n        {\"Kerem\", {12, 4, 2006}, 82},\n        {\"Merve\", {19, 7, 2005}, 91}\n    };\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 2; i++) {\n        printStudent(cls[i]);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n<p>When you pass a struct to a function by value (<code>void printStudent(Student s)</code>), the function receives a copy. To modify the original struct inside a function, pass its address (<code>Student *p</code>) and use <code>p-&gt;grade</code> (which is shorthand for <code>(*p).grade</code>).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Edit the program below to add a 3rd student, compute the average grade of all 3 students, and print it with 1 decimal place.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    char name[20];\n    int grade;\n} Student;\n\nint main() {\n    Student list[3] = {\n        {\"Deniz\", 80},\n        {\"Ece\", 92},\n        {\"Bora\", 74}\n    };\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n        sum += list[i].grade;\n    }\n    printf(\"Average: %.1f\\n\", sum / 3.0);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the semicolon <code>;</code> after the closing brace <code>}</code> of a <code>struct</code> definition.</li><li>Trying to assign a string to a struct field with <code>s.name = \"Ali\"</code> instead of <code>strcpy(s.name, \"Ali\")</code>.</li><li>Passing a struct by value when you meant to change its fields inside the function — pass a pointer <code>Student *p</code> and use <code>-&gt;</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>struct</strong></td><td>a composite type grouping fields of different types</td><td lang=\"tr\">yapı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>typedef</strong></td><td>creates a shorter alias name for a type</td><td lang=\"tr\">tür takma adı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>member (field)</strong></td><td>one variable inside a struct, accessed with <code>.</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">üye (alan)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>arrow operator (-&gt;)</strong></td><td>accesses a struct field through a pointer</td><td lang=\"tr\">ok operatörü</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is missing at the end of <code>struct Point { int x; int y; }</code>?","opts":[{"h":"A colon <code>:</code>","why":"C does not end struct definitions with a colon."},{"h":"A semicolon <code>;</code>","ok":1,"why":"Every struct definition in C must end with <code>;</code>."},{"h":"A comma <code>,</code>","why":"Commas separate variables, not the end of a definition."},{"h":"Nothing is missing","why":"Without <code>;</code>, the compiler treats the next token as a variable name."}],"why":"A struct definition always ends with <code>};</code>."},{"q":"If <code>p</code> is a <code>Student *</code> pointer, how do you access the <code>gpa</code> field?","opts":[{"h":"<code>p.gpa</code>","why":"Dot <code>.</code> works on a struct value, not on a pointer."},{"h":"<code>p-&gt;gpa</code>","ok":1,"why":"The arrow operator <code>-&gt;</code> dereferences the pointer and reads the field."},{"h":"<code>*p.gpa</code>","why":"Dot binds tighter than <code>*</code>, so <code>*p.gpa</code> tries to dereference <code>gpa</code>."},{"h":"<code>p&amp;gpa</code>","why":"<code>&amp;</code> is the address-of operator, not member access."}],"why":"Use <code>-&gt;</code> on a pointer to a struct (<code>p-&gt;gpa</code> is the same as <code>(*p).gpa</code>)."},{"q":"Which function copies <code>\"Nisa\"</code> into <code>s.name</code> after <code>s</code> is already declared?","opts":[{"h":"<code>s.name = \"Nisa\";</code>","why":"Arrays in C cannot be assigned with <code>=</code>."},{"h":"<code>strcpy(s.name, \"Nisa\");</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>strcpy</code> from <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code> copies characters into a char array."},{"h":"<code>strcmp(s.name, \"Nisa\");</code>","why":"<code>strcmp</code> compares two strings; it does not copy."},{"h":"<code>strlen(s.name, \"Nisa\");</code>","why":"<code>strlen</code> takes one string and returns its length."}],"why":"Use <code>strcpy</code> (from <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code>) to copy a string into a struct's <code>char</code> array."}]],"id":"bridge-02","title":"Preview: C structs","goal":"group related variables into a struct, use arrays of structs and pass structs to functions","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"struct","m":"a composite type grouping fields of different types","tr":"yapı"},{"t":"typedef","m":"creates a shorter alias name for a type","tr":"tür takma adı"},{"t":"member (field)","m":"one variable inside a struct, accessed with `.`","tr":"üye (alan)"},{"t":"arrow operator (->)","m":"accesses a struct field through a pointer","tr":"ok operatörü"}]},"bridge-03":{"html":"<p>Variables in RAM disappear when your program ends. <strong>Programming Basics II</strong> teaches you how to save records to a file on disk and read them back later.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-opening-writing-and-closing-a-file\"><h2 id=\"opening-writing-and-closing-a-file\">Opening, writing and closing a file</h2>\n<p>Every file operation follows four steps:</p>\n<ol><li>Declare a <code>FILE *</code> pointer.</li><li>Call <code>fopen(\"filename\", \"mode\")</code> (<code>\"w\"</code> writes a new file, <code>\"a\"</code> appends to the end, <code>\"r\"</code> reads).</li><li>Check if the pointer is <code>NULL</code> (meaning the file could not be opened).</li><li>Write or read, then call <code>fclose(fp)</code>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"grades.txt\", \"w\");\n    if (fp == NULL) {\n        printf(\"Could not open file.\\n\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    fprintf(fp, \"Deniz 85\\n\");\n    fprintf(fp, \"Ece 94\\n\");\n    fclose(fp);\n\n    fp = fopen(\"grades.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (fp == NULL) {\n        printf(\"Could not open file for reading.\\n\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    char name[30];\n    int score;\n    while (fscanf(fp, \"%s %d\", name, &amp;score) == 2) {\n        printf(\"%s scored %d\\n\", name, score);\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-line-by-line-with-fgets\"><h2 id=\"reading-line-by-line-with-fgets\">Reading line by line with <code>fgets</code></h2>\n<p>When lines contain spaces, <code>fgets(buffer, size, fp)</code> is safer than <code>fscanf</code>, because it reads up to <code>\\n</code> (and stops before overflowing the buffer).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"w\");\n    fprintf(fp, \"Line one: C files\\nLine two: always close!\\n\");\n    fclose(fp);\n\n    fp = fopen(\"notes.txt\", \"r\");\n    char line[100];\n    while (fgets(line, 100, fp) != NULL) {\n        printf(\"&gt; %s\", line);\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Modify the program below to append a 3rd student (<code>\"Kerem\"</code>, <code>79</code>) using mode <code>\"a\"</code>, then read and print all lines.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"crun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"class.txt\", \"w\");\n    fprintf(fp, \"Deniz 85\\nEce 94\\n\");\n    fclose(fp);\n\n    fp = fopen(\"class.txt\", \"a\");\n    if (fp != NULL) {\n        fprintf(fp, \"Kerem 79\\n\");\n        fclose(fp);\n    }\n\n    fp = fopen(\"class.txt\", \"r\");\n    char name[30];\n    int g;\n    while (fscanf(fp, \"%s %d\", name, &amp;g) == 2) {\n        printf(\"%s -&gt; %d\\n\", name, g);\n    }\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using the file pointer without checking <code>if (fp == NULL)</code> first — if the file doesn't exist in <code>\"r\"</code> mode, reading from <code>NULL</code> crashes the program.</li><li>Opening an existing file with <code>\"w\"</code> when you meant <code>\"a\"</code> — <code>\"w\"</code> erases the old contents immediately.</li><li>Forgetting <code>fclose(fp)</code>, which can leave written data stuck in a buffer instead of saved on disk.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>FILE pointer</strong></td><td>a <code>FILE *</code> handle returned by <code>fopen</code> to track an open file</td><td lang=\"tr\">dosya işaretçisi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>file mode</strong></td><td><code>\"r\"</code> read, <code>\"w\"</code> write/overwrite, <code>\"a\"</code> append</td><td lang=\"tr\">dosya modu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>buffer</strong></td><td>a temporary char array used to hold a line read from a file</td><td lang=\"tr\">tampon bellek</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does <code>fopen(\"data.txt\", \"w\")</code> do if <code>data.txt</code> already exists?","opts":[{"h":"Adds new lines to the end","why":"Appending is mode <code>\"a\"</code>."},{"h":"Erases the existing contents and starts empty","ok":1,"why":"Mode <code>\"w\"</code> truncates an existing file to zero length."},{"h":"Returns <code>NULL</code>","why":"Mode <code>\"w\"</code> creates or overwrites; <code>\"r\"</code> returns <code>NULL</code> if missing."},{"h":"Opens it read-only","why":"Read-only is mode <code>\"r\"</code>."}],"why":"<code>\"w\"</code> creates a new file or overwrites an existing one. Use <code>\"a\"</code> to keep old data."},{"q":"What does <code>fopen(\"missing.txt\", \"r\")</code> return if the file does not exist?","opts":[{"h":"An empty string <code>\"\"</code>","why":"<code>fopen</code> returns a pointer, not a string."},{"h":"<code>0</code> as an integer error code","why":"It returns a pointer, specifically <code>NULL</code>."},{"h":"<code>NULL</code>","ok":1,"why":"Always check <code>if (fp == NULL)</code> after <code>fopen</code>."},{"h":"It creates an empty file","why":"Only <code>\"w\"</code> and <code>\"a\"</code> create a missing file."}],"why":"Reading a missing file with <code>\"r\"</code> returns <code>NULL</code>."},{"q":"Why is <code>while (fscanf(fp, \"%s %d\", name, &amp;g) == 2)</code> better than <code>while (!feof(fp))</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>feof</code> only works for binary files","why":"<code>feof</code> works on any stream, but only <em>after</em> a read fails."},{"h":"<code>fscanf</code> returns how many items were actually read, preventing an extra duplicate loop at the end","ok":1,"why":"<code>feof</code> only becomes true after trying to read past the end."},{"h":"<code>feof</code> deletes the last line","why":"<code>feof</code> does not modify the file."},{"h":"<code>fscanf</code> automatically closes the file","why":"You still must call <code>fclose(fp)</code>."}],"why":"Checking the return value of <code>fscanf</code> or <code>fgets</code> avoids reading garbage after the last line."}]],"id":"bridge-03","title":"Preview: C file operations","goal":"open, write, read and close text files in C using fopen, fprintf, fgets and fclose","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"FILE pointer","m":"a `FILE *` handle returned by `fopen` to track an open file","tr":"dosya işaretçisi"},{"t":"file mode","m":"`\"r\"` read, `\"w\"` write/overwrite, `\"a\"` append","tr":"dosya modu"},{"t":"buffer","m":"a temporary char array used to hold a line read from a file","tr":"tampon bellek"}]},"bridge-04":{"html":"<p><strong>Object Oriented Programming (CMP157)</strong> in semester 2 uses <strong>Java</strong> (7 ECTS). Good news: Java copied much of its syntax from C, so <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>switch</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, operators and braces <code>{ }</code> work almost identically.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-side-by-side-c-vs-java\"><h2 id=\"side-by-side-c-vs-java\">Side by side: C vs Java</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>C</th><th>Java</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry point</td><td><code>int main()</code></td><td><code>public static void main(String[] args)</code> inside a <code>class</code></td></tr><tr><td>Print to screen</td><td><code>printf(\"Hi %d\\n\", x);</code></td><td><code>System.out.println(\"Hi \" + x);</code></td></tr><tr><td>Read from keyboard</td><td><code>scanf(\"%d\", &amp;x);</code></td><td><code>Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in); int x = sc.nextInt();</code></td></tr><tr><td>Strings</td><td><code>char s[20];</code> with <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code></td><td>Built-in <code>String s = \"Ali\";</code> (and <code>s.equals(\"Ali\")</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Booleans</td><td><code>0</code> (false) / non-zero (true)</td><td>Dedicated <code>boolean</code> (<code>true</code> / <code>false</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td>Standalone functions</td><td><strong>Methods</strong> inside a <code>class</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-same-program-in-c-and-java\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-same-program-in-c-and-java\">Worked example: same program in C and Java</h2>\n<p>In C:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"c\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int nums[3] = {10, 20, 30};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n        printf(\"%d\\n\", add(nums[i], 5));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>In Java:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"java\"><code>public class Main {\n    static int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] nums = {10, 20, 30};\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; nums.length; i++) {\n            System.out.println(add(nums[i], 5));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Notice two handy improvements in Java:</p>\n<ol><li>Java arrays know their own length (<code>nums.length</code>), so you don't need a separate <code>n</code> variable.</li><li>Java strings are real objects (<code>String</code>), so you join them with <code>+</code> and compare them with <code>.equals(...)</code> (never <code>==</code>).</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Check your understanding of the C → Java differences:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Comparing Java <code>String</code> values with <code>==</code> instead of <code>.equals(...)</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>if (x)</code> where <code>x</code> is an <code>int</code> — Java requires an explicit <code>boolean</code> condition like <code>if (x != 0)</code>.</li><li>Writing <code>arr.length()</code> with parentheses on an array (<code>length</code> is a field on arrays, whereas <code>length()</code> is a method on <code>String</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>method</strong></td><td>a function defined inside a Java class</td><td lang=\"tr\">metot (yöntem)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>String</strong></td><td>Java's built-in text type (immutable object, not a char array)</td><td lang=\"tr\">metin türü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Scanner</strong></td><td>Java utility class (<code>java.util.Scanner</code>) for reading keyboard input</td><td lang=\"tr\">tarayıcı sınıfı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>boolean</strong></td><td>a Java data type with only <code>true</code> and <code>false</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">mantıksal tür</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which Java statement prints <code>Score: 90</code> followed by a new line?","opts":[{"h":"<code>printf(\"Score: %d\\n\", 90);</code>","why":"<code>printf</code> is C (Java uses <code>System.out.printf</code> or <code>System.out.println</code>)."},{"h":"<code>System.out.println(\"Score: \" + 90);</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>println</code> prints the text and moves to a new line."},{"h":"<code>System.in.println(\"Score: \" + 90);</code>","why":"<code>System.in</code> is for input, not output."},{"h":"<code>print(\"Score: \" + 90);</code>","why":"Java has no global <code>print</code> function."}],"why":"<code>System.out.println(...)</code> is Java's standard line-printing call."},{"q":"What happens if you write <code>int x = 5; if (x) { ... }</code> in Java?","opts":[{"h":"It runs the <code>if</code> body because 5 is non-zero","why":"That is C behaviour, not Java."},{"h":"It fails to compile because <code>if</code> requires a <code>boolean</code>","ok":1,"why":"Java does not treat integers as true/false."},{"h":"It skips the <code>if</code> body","why":"It does not compile at all."},{"h":"It converts <code>x</code> to <code>\"5\"</code>","why":"No automatic conversion to boolean happens."}],"why":"Java <code>if</code> conditions must evaluate to <code>boolean</code> (<code>true</code> or <code>false</code>)."},{"q":"How do you read an <code>int</code> from <code>Scanner sc</code> in Java?","opts":[{"h":"<code>sc.scanf(\"%d\")</code>","why":"<code>scanf</code> is C, not a <code>Scanner</code> method."},{"h":"<code>sc.nextInt()</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>nextInt()</code> reads the next integer token."},{"h":"<code>sc.readInt()</code>","why":"That is not a <code>Scanner</code> method name."},{"h":"<code>sc.get()</code>","why":"That is not a <code>Scanner</code> method."}],"why":"<code>Scanner</code> provides <code>nextInt()</code>, <code>nextDouble()</code>, <code>next()</code>, and <code>nextLine()</code>."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["In Java, every <code>main</code> method must sit inside a ","."],"a":[["class"]],"note":"Java has no standalone functions outside classes."},{"t":["To get the number of elements in a Java array <code>arr</code>, write ","."],"a":[["arr.length"]],"note":"No parentheses on array <code>.length</code>."},{"t":["To compare two Java <code>String</code> variables <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> for equal text, write ","."],"a":[["a.equals(b)","b.equals(a)"]],"note":"<code>==</code> checks whether two references point to the same object, not text equality."},{"t":["In Java, a <code>boolean</code> variable can only hold <code>true</code> or ","."],"a":[["false"]],"note":"Unlike C, Java <code>if (1)</code> does not compile; conditions must be <code>boolean</code>."}]],"id":"bridge-04","title":"Preview: From C to Java","goal":"translate basic C programs (types, I/O, conditions, loops, arrays, functions) into Java","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"method","m":"a function defined inside a Java class","tr":"metot (yöntem)"},{"t":"String","m":"Java's built-in text type (immutable object, not a char array)","tr":"metin türü"},{"t":"Scanner","m":"Java utility class (`java.util.Scanner`) for reading keyboard input","tr":"tarayıcı sınıfı"},{"t":"boolean","m":"a Java data type with only `true` and `false`","tr":"mantıksal tür"}]},"bridge-05":{"html":"<p>In <strong>Object Oriented Programming (CMP157)</strong>, the university uses <strong>Java</strong> and the <strong>Eclipse IDE</strong>. Instead of writing loose functions and variables, you group data and behaviour together into <strong>classes</strong> and create <strong>objects</strong> from them.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-installing-jdk-and-eclipse-before-week-1\"><h2 id=\"installing-jdk-and-eclipse-before-week-1\">Installing JDK and Eclipse (before week 1)</h2>\n<ol><li>Install a <strong>JDK (Java Development Kit)</strong> — e.g. Eclipse Temurin (Java 17 or 21 LTS). The JDK includes <code>javac</code> (the compiler) and <code>java</code> (the runner).</li><li>Download <strong>Eclipse IDE for Java Developers</strong> from <code>eclipse.org</code>.</li><li>In Eclipse: <strong>File › New › Java Project</strong>, give it a name, then right-click <code>src</code> › <strong>New › Class</strong> (tick <code>public static void main</code> for your starter class).</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-classes-objects-and-constructors\"><h2 id=\"classes-objects-and-constructors\">Classes, objects and constructors</h2>\n<p>A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint (like a <code>struct</code> in C, plus methods). An <strong>object</strong> is one actual instance built from that blueprint using <code>new</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"java\"><code>public class Student {\n    // Fields (data)\n    private String name;\n    private double gpa;\n\n    // Constructor: runs when you create a new Student\n    public Student(String name, double gpa) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.gpa = gpa;\n    }\n\n    // Method (behaviour)\n    public void printInfo() {\n        System.out.println(name + \" — GPA: \" + gpa);\n    }\n\n    public boolean isHonor() {\n        return gpa &gt;= 3.5;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student(\"Deniz\", 3.65);\n        Student s2 = new Student(\"Kerem\", 2.90);\n\n        s1.printInfo();\n        System.out.println(\"Honor? \" + s1.isHonor());\n        s2.printInfo();\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-access-modifiers-at-a-glance\"><h2 id=\"access-modifiers-at-a-glance\">Access modifiers at a glance</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Modifier</th><th>Who can see it?</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>Any class anywhere</td><td>Constructors, methods meant for callers</td></tr><tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>Only inside the same class</td><td>Fields (to protect data — <strong>encapsulation</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>protected</code></td><td>Same package + subclasses</td><td>Inheritance (covered later in OOP)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Fill in the blanks for a <code>Book</code> class:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Giving a constructor a return type like <code>void Student(...)</code> — that turns it into a normal method, not a constructor.</li><li>Naming the <code>.java</code> file differently from the <code>public class</code> inside it (<code>Student.java</code> must contain <code>public class Student</code>).</li><li>Forgetting <code>new</code> when creating an object (<code>Student s1;</code> only declares a <code>null</code> reference).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>class</strong></td><td>a blueprint defining fields and methods for objects</td><td lang=\"tr\">sınıf</td></tr><tr><td><strong>object</strong></td><td>a concrete instance of a class created with <code>new</code></td><td lang=\"tr\">nesne</td></tr><tr><td><strong>constructor</strong></td><td>a special method that initializes a new object</td><td lang=\"tr\">yapıcı metot (constructor)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>encapsulation</strong></td><td>hiding <code>private</code> fields and exposing controlled <code>public</code> methods</td><td lang=\"tr\">kapsülleme</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What happens if you write <code>Student s; s.printInfo();</code> without <code>new Student(...)</code>?","opts":[{"h":"It prints empty values","why":"No object was created yet."},{"h":"It fails to compile (local variable not initialized) or throws a <code>NullPointerException</code>","ok":1,"why":"A reference variable without <code>new</code> does not point to any object."},{"h":"Java creates a default <code>Student</code> automatically","why":"Java only creates objects when you call <code>new</code>."},{"h":"It calls the constructor twice","why":"No constructor ran."}],"why":"You must create an object with <code>new</code> before calling methods on it."},{"q":"Why are fields usually marked <code>private</code> in Java?","opts":[{"h":"So Java runs faster","why":"Access modifiers are compile-time visibility rules, not speed optimizations."},{"h":"To protect the object's data from invalid outside changes","ok":1,"why":"Callers use <code>public</code> getter/setter or action methods instead."},{"h":"Because <code>public</code> fields cannot hold numbers","why":"<code>public</code> works with any type."},{"h":"So <code>main</code> cannot read them","why":"<code>main</code> in the same class can read them, and other classes use <code>public</code> methods."}],"why":"<code>private</code> fields + <code>public</code> methods is the basis of encapsulation."},{"q":"Which of these is a valid constructor header for <code>class Course</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>public void Course(String title)</code>","why":"Constructors have NO return type, not even <code>void</code>."},{"h":"<code>public Course(String title)</code>","ok":1,"why":"Same name as the class, no return type."},{"h":"<code>public int Course()</code>","why":"<code>int</code> is a return type, making it a method."},{"h":"<code>constructor Course()</code>","why":"Java does not use a <code>constructor</code> keyword."}],"why":"A constructor has the exact class name and no return type."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["To create a new <code>Student</code> object named <code>s</code>, you use the keyword ","."],"a":[["new"]],"note":"<code>new</code> allocates the object and calls its constructor."},{"t":["A "," has the same name as the class and no return type; it initializes the object's fields."],"a":[["constructor"]],"note":"Constructors run automatically on <code>new</code>."},{"t":["Marking fields <code>private</code> so outside code cannot change them directly is called ","."],"a":[["encapsulation"]],"note":"Encapsulation hides internal state behind public methods."},{"t":["Inside a constructor, `","` refers to the current object being created."],"a":[["this"]],"note":"<code>this.name = name;</code> distinguishes the field from the parameter."}]],"id":"bridge-05","title":"Preview: JDK, Eclipse and your first Java class","goal":"understand how to set up Java + Eclipse and write a basic class with fields, a constructor and a method","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"class","m":"a blueprint defining fields and methods for objects","tr":"sınıf"},{"t":"object","m":"a concrete instance of a class created with `new`","tr":"nesne"},{"t":"constructor","m":"a special method that initializes a new object","tr":"yapıcı metot (constructor)"},{"t":"encapsulation","m":"hiding `private` fields and exposing controlled `public` methods","tr":"kapsülleme"}]},"bridge-06":{"html":"<p><strong>Database Management Systems II (CMP152)</strong> (7 ECTS) starts by reviewing ER diagrams and normalization, then moves straight into <strong>SQL functions, views, transactions and stored procedures</strong> (in T-SQL / SQL Server).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-built-in-sql-functions\"><h2 id=\"built-in-sql-functions\">Built-in SQL functions</h2>\n<p>You can transform column values right inside <code>SELECT</code>:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>String:</strong> <code>UPPER(s)</code>, <code>LOWER(s)</code>, <code>LENGTH(s)</code>, <code>SUBSTR(s, start, len)</code></li><li><strong>Math:</strong> <code>ROUND(x, n)</code>, <code>ABS(x)</code></li><li><strong>Date:</strong> <code>date('now')</code>, <code>strftime('%Y', birth_date)</code></li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT\n    UPPER(last_name) || ', ' || first_name AS full_name,\n    LENGTH(email) AS email_len,\n    ROUND(gpa, 1) AS rounded_gpa,\n    strftime('%Y', birth_date) AS birth_year\nFROM Students\nWHERE gpa IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY gpa DESC\nLIMIT 8;</pre></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tsql\"><div class=\"callout-h\">SQL Server (T-SQL) equivalents</div><ul><li><code>LENGTH(s)</code> → <code>LEN(s)</code></li><li><code>SUBSTR(s, 1, 3)</code> → <code>SUBSTRING(s, 1, 3)</code></li><li><code>a || b</code> (string concat) → <code>a + b</code> or <code>CONCAT(a, b)</code></li><li><code>date('now')</code> → <code>GETDATE()</code></li><li><code>strftime('%Y', birth_date)</code> → <code>YEAR(birth_date)</code></li><li><code>LIMIT 8</code> → <code>SELECT TOP 8 ...</code></li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-views-create-view\"><h2 id=\"views-create-view\">Views (<code>CREATE VIEW</code>)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>view</strong> is a saved <code>SELECT</code> query with a name. You can query it like a table, without duplicating data.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">CREATE VIEW IF NOT EXISTS HonorStudents AS\nSELECT s.student_id, s.first_name, s.last_name, d.dept_name, s.gpa\nFROM Students s\nJOIN Departments d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id\nWHERE s.gpa &gt;= 3.50;\n\nSELECT first_name, last_name, dept_name, gpa\nFROM HonorStudents\nORDER BY gpa DESC;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-stored-procedures-sql-server-t-sql\"><h2 id=\"stored-procedures-sql-server-t-sql\">Stored procedures (SQL Server / T-SQL)</h2>\n<p>SQLite does not have stored procedures, so you will write these in SQL Server in semester 2. A <strong>stored procedure</strong> is a saved block of SQL statements (often with parameters) that lives in the database and runs when called with <code>EXEC</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><code>-- T-SQL (SQL Server)\nCREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentsByCity\n    @City NVARCHAR(50)\nAS\nBEGIN\n    SELECT first_name, last_name, gpa\n    FROM Students\n    WHERE city = @City\n    ORDER BY last_name;\nEND;\n\n-- Run it later:\nEXEC GetStudentsByCity @City = 'Istanbul';</code></pre>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Edit the query below to list each course's <code>course_code</code>, its <code>title</code> in uppercase, and the average <code>grade</code> rounded to 1 decimal place (<code>ROUND(AVG(grade), 1)</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"sqlrun\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">SELECT\n    c.course_code,\n    UPPER(c.title) AS course_title,\n    ROUND(AVG(e.grade), 1) AS avg_grade\nFROM Courses c\nJOIN Enrollments e ON c.course_id = e.course_id\nWHERE e.grade IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY c.course_id, c.course_code, c.title\nORDER BY avg_grade DESC;</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting a view's <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>CREATE VIEW</code> in SQL Server without <code>TOP</code> (SQL Server forbids <code>ORDER BY</code> inside a view definition unless <code>TOP</code> is used — put <code>ORDER BY</code> when selecting from the view).</li><li>Forgetting that <code>NULL</code> values pass through string/math functions as <code>NULL</code> (e.g. <code>LENGTH(NULL)</code> is <code>NULL</code>).</li><li>Confusing a <strong>view</strong> (a virtual table from a <code>SELECT</code>) with a <strong>stored procedure</strong> (a callable program of SQL statements with parameters).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>scalar function</strong></td><td>a function that runs on each row and returns one value (e.g. <code>UPPER</code>, <code>ROUND</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">skaler fonksiyon</td></tr><tr><td><strong>view</strong></td><td>a saved <code>SELECT</code> query queried like a table</td><td lang=\"tr\">görünüm (view)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stored procedure</strong></td><td>a named, parameterized SQL script saved in the database</td><td lang=\"tr\">saklı yordam (stored procedure)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Does <code>CREATE VIEW</code> copy the table's rows into a second table on disk?","opts":[{"h":"Yes, it duplicates all rows","why":"A standard view stores only the <code>SELECT</code> definition, not a copy of the data."},{"h":"No, it stores the query and reads live data from the underlying tables","ok":1,"why":"Updating a base table updates what the view shows."},{"h":"Only if <code>WHERE</code> is used","why":"<code>WHERE</code> just filters the query."},{"h":"Yes, until you run <code>DROP TABLE</code>","why":"You drop a view with <code>DROP VIEW</code>."}],"why":"A view is a virtual table: it stores the query, not a separate copy of the rows."},{"q":"Which T-SQL function gives the current date and time in SQL Server?","opts":[{"h":"<code>date('now')</code>","why":"That is SQLite syntax."},{"h":"<code>GETDATE()</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>GETDATE()</code> is SQL Server's current date/time function."},{"h":"<code>LENGTH()</code>","why":"<code>LENGTH</code> measures string length in SQLite."},{"h":"<code>TODAY()</code>","why":"Not a built-in SQL Server function."}],"why":"In SQL Server (T-SQL), use <code>GETDATE()</code> (and <code>YEAR(d)</code> / <code>MONTH(d)</code> to extract parts)."},{"q":"How do you run a stored procedure named <code>GetStudentsByCity</code> in SQL Server?","opts":[{"h":"<code>SELECT * FROM GetStudentsByCity;</code>","why":"<code>SELECT * FROM</code> is for tables and views."},{"h":"<code>EXEC GetStudentsByCity @City = 'Istanbul';</code>","ok":1,"why":"Stored procedures are invoked with <code>EXEC</code> (or <code>EXECUTE</code>)."},{"h":"<code>RUN GetStudentsByCity;</code>","why":"<code>RUN</code> is not a SQL keyword."},{"h":"<code>CALL VIEW GetStudentsByCity;</code>","why":"Wrong keywords."}],"why":"Stored procedures in T-SQL are executed with <code>EXEC</code>."}]],"id":"bridge-06","title":"Preview: SQL functions, views and stored procedures","goal":"use built-in string/date/math SQL functions, create a view, and understand what a stored procedure does","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"scalar function","m":"a function that runs on each row and returns one value (e.g. `UPPER`, `ROUND`)","tr":"skaler fonksiyon"},{"t":"view","m":"a saved `SELECT` query queried like a table","tr":"görünüm (view)"},{"t":"stored procedure","m":"a named, parameterized SQL script saved in the database","tr":"saklı yordam (stored procedure)"}]},"bridge-07":{"html":"<p><strong>Mathematics II (MTH154)</strong> moves from algebra into <strong>functions, logarithms, trigonometry, limits and calculus</strong>. Everything starts with the idea of a <strong>function</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-what-is-a-function\"><h2 id=\"what-is-a-function\">What is a function?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>function</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f\"></span> is a rule that gives <strong>exactly one</strong> output <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x)\"></span> for each allowed input <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Domain (<em>tanım kümesi</em>):</strong> all allowed input values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span>.</li><li><strong>Range (<em>görüntü kümesi</em>):</strong> all actual output values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x)\"></span>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = 2x + 3\"></span>, then: $<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(4) = 2(4) + 3 = 11\"></span>$</p>\n<p>Two restrictions to watch for when finding the domain of real functions:</p>\n<ol><li>You <strong>cannot divide by zero</strong>: in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 5}\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x \\neq 5\"></span>, so the domain is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{5\\}\"></span>.</li><li>You <strong>cannot take an even root of a negative number</strong>: in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h(x) = \\sqrt{x - 2}\"></span>, we need <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x - 2 \\ge 0\"></span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x \\ge 2\"></span>, i.e. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"[2, \\infty)\"></span>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-linear-and-quadratic-graphs\"><h2 id=\"linear-and-quadratic-graphs\">Linear and quadratic graphs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Linear:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = mx + b\"></span> is a straight line with <strong>slope</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"m\"></span> and <strong>y-intercept</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(0, b)\"></span>.</li><li><strong>Quadratic:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c\"></span> is a <strong>parabola</strong>. It opens upward if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"a &gt; 0\"></span> and downward if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"a &lt; 0\"></span>. Its vertex (<em>tepe noktası</em>) is at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = -\\frac{b}{2a}\"></span>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-composition-and-inverse-functions\"><h2 id=\"composition-and-inverse-functions\">Composition and inverse functions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Composition:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x))\"></span> — apply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g\"></span> first, then plug the result into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f\"></span>. - If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = x^2\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g(x) = x + 1\"></span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(g(3)) = f(4) = 16\"></span>.</li><li><strong>Inverse:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x)\"></span> undoes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f\"></span>. To find it, write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y = f(x)\"></span>, swap <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y\"></span>, and solve for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y\"></span>. - For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = 2x - 6\"></span>: swap to get <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 2y - 6 \\implies y = \\frac{x + 6}{2}\"></span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x + 6}{2}\"></span>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Work out each question, then open the answer to check.</p>\n<ol><li>Find the domain of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{x + 1}}{x - 3}\"></span>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>We need <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x + 1 \\ge 0 \\implies x \\ge -1\"></span>, AND <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x - 3 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq 3\"></span>. So the domain is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"[-1, \\infty) \\setminus \\{3\\}\"></span>.</p></div></details>\n<ol><li>Given <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = 3x - 2\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g(x) = x^2 + 1\"></span>, find <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(f \\circ g)(2)\"></span>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>First <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g(2) = 2^2 + 1 = 5\"></span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(5) = 3(5) - 2 = 13\"></span>.</p></div></details>\n<ol><li>Find <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x)\"></span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = \\frac{x - 4}{5}\"></span>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y = \\frac{x - 4}{5}\"></span>, swap <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = \\frac{y - 4}{5} \\implies 5x = y - 4 \\implies y = 5x + 4\"></span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x) = 5x + 4\"></span>.</p></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{1}{f(x)}\"></span> — the superscript <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-1\"></span> on a function means <strong>inverse function</strong>, not reciprocal!</li><li>Computing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(f \\circ g)(x)\"></span> in the wrong order: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(g(x))\"></span> applies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"g\"></span> first, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f\"></span>.</li><li>Forgetting to exclude zeros of the denominator from the domain.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>function</strong></td><td>a rule assigning each input <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span> exactly one output <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x)\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">fonksiyon</td></tr><tr><td><strong>domain</strong></td><td>the set of all valid input values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">tanım kümesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>range</strong></td><td>the set of all output values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x)\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">görüntü kümesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>inverse function</strong></td><td>the function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}\"></span> that reverses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(f(x)) = x\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">ters fonksiyon</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = x^2 - 3x\"></span>, what is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(-2)\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-2\"></span>","why":"Check the sign of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-2)^2\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-3(-2)\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-2)^2 = +4\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-3(-2) = +6\"></span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-2\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"10\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-2)^2 - 3(-2) = 4 + 6 = 10\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-10\"></span>","why":"Both terms are positive when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = -2\"></span>."}],"why":"Substitute <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-2\"></span> carefully with parentheses: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-2)^2 - 3(-2) = 4 + 6 = 10\"></span>."},{"q":"What is the domain of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = \\sqrt{6 - 2x}\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"[3, \\infty)\"></span>","why":"For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 4\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"6 - 8 = -2 &lt; 0\"></span>, which is not allowed."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-\\infty, 3]\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"We need <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"6 - 2x \\ge 0 \\implies 6 \\ge 2x \\implies x \\le 3\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{3\\}\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 3\"></span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\sqrt{0} = 0\"></span>, which is valid; values above 3 fail."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(-\\infty, 3)\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 3\"></span> is allowed because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\sqrt{0} = 0\"></span>."}],"why":"For a square root, set the inside <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\ge 0\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"6 - 2x \\ge 0 \\implies x \\le 3\"></span>."},{"q":"If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f(x) = 4x + 8\"></span>, what is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"f^{-1}(x)\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\frac{1}{4x + 8}\"></span>","why":"That is the reciprocal, not the inverse function."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\frac{x - 8}{4}\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"Swap <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 4y + 8 \\implies y = \\frac{x - 8}{4}\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4x - 8\"></span>","why":"You must divide by 4 as well as subtract 8."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\frac{x}{4} + 8\"></span>","why":"Subtract 8 before dividing by 4."}],"why":"Swap <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"y\"></span> and solve: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 4y + 8 \\implies y = (x - 8)/4\"></span>."}]],"id":"bridge-07","title":"Preview: Functions and graphs (Math II)","goal":"evaluate functions, find domain and range, and sketch linear and quadratic graphs","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"function","m":"a rule assigning each input $x$ exactly one output $f(x)$","tr":"fonksiyon"},{"t":"domain","m":"the set of all valid input values $x$","tr":"tanım kümesi"},{"t":"range","m":"the set of all output values $f(x)$","tr":"görüntü kümesi"},{"t":"inverse function","m":"the function $f^{-1}$ that reverses $f$: $f^{-1}(f(x)) = x$","tr":"ters fonksiyon"}]},"bridge-08":{"html":"<p>A <strong>logarithm</strong> answers one question: <em>\"What power must I raise the base to, to get this number?\"</em> It is the inverse of an exponential function.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-definition\"><h2 id=\"definition\">Definition</h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"a &gt; 0\"></span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"a \\neq 1\"></span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x &gt; 0\"></span>: $<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(x) = y \\iff a^y = x\"></span>$</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_2(8) = 3\"></span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^3 = 8\"></span>.</li><li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_{10}(100) = 2\"></span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"10^2 = 100\"></span>. (Base 10 is often written simply as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log x\"></span>.)</li><li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(1) = 0\"></span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"5^0 = 1\"></span>.</li><li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_3\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right) = -2\"></span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3^{-2} = \\frac{1}{9}\"></span>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-4-key-logarithm-rules\"><h2 id=\"the-4-key-logarithm-rules\">The 4 key logarithm rules</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Product rule:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(x \\cdot y) = \\log_a x + \\log_a y\"></span></li><li><strong>Quotient rule:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) = \\log_a x - \\log_a y\"></span></li><li><strong>Power rule:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(x^k) = k \\cdot \\log_a x\"></span></li><li><strong>Change of base:</strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\"></span> (especially <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a b = \\frac{1}{\\log_b a}\"></span>)</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Simplify <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_2(12) + \\log_2(4) - \\log_2(6)\"></span>.</p>\n<ul><li>Combine using product and quotient rules: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_2\\left(\\frac{12 \\cdot 4}{6}\\right) = \\log_2(8) = 3\"></span>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Solve these, then reveal the solutions:</p>\n<ol><li>Evaluate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_3(81) + \\log_2\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)\"></span>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3^4 = 81 \\implies \\log_3(81) = 4\"></span>. And <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^{-3} = \\frac{1}{8} \\implies \\log_2\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) = -3\"></span>. Total: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4 + (-3) = 1\"></span>.</p></div></details>\n<ol><li>Solve for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_2(x - 1) = 4\"></span>.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Rewrite in exponential form: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x - 1 = 2^4 = 16 \\implies x = 17\"></span>. (Check: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"17 - 1 = 16 &gt; 0\"></span>, valid!)</p></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(x + y) = \\log_a x + \\log_a y\"></span> — addition inside a log does <strong>not</strong> split! Only multiplication inside splits into addition outside.</li><li>Forgetting that the argument of a logarithm must be strictly positive (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x &gt; 0\"></span>); always check solutions to log equations.</li><li>Confusing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(1) = 0\"></span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(a) = 1\"></span>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>logarithm</strong></td><td>the exponent needed to reach a number from a given base</td><td lang=\"tr\">logaritma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>base</strong></td><td>the number <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"a\"></span> being raised to a power in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_a(x)\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">taban</td></tr><tr><td><strong>natural logarithm</strong></td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\ln(x) = \\log_e(x)\"></span> where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"e \\approx 2.718\"></span></td><td lang=\"tr\">doğal logaritma</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_4(64)\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4^2 = 16\"></span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"64\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4^3 = 64\"></span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_4(64) = 3\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4^4 = 256\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"16\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"64 / 4 = 16\"></span>, not the exponent."}],"why":"Ask: 4 to what power equals 64? <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"4^3 = 64\"></span>, so the answer is 3."},{"q":"Which expression equals <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(25x^3)\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2 + 3\\log_5(x)\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(25) + \\log_5(x^3) = 2 + 3\\log_5(x)\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2 \\cdot 3\\log_5(x)\"></span>","why":"Product inside a log becomes addition, not multiplication."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"5 + 3\\log_5(x)\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(25) = 2\"></span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"5\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"25 + 3\\log_5(x)\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(25) = 2\"></span>, not <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"25\"></span>."}],"why":"Split the product into a sum, evaluate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_5(25) = 2\"></span>, and bring the exponent 3 to the front."},{"q":"Solve for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\log_3(x) = -2\"></span>.","opts":[{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-6\"></span>","why":"A logarithm is an exponent, not multiplication."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-9\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3^{-2}\"></span> is positive, not negative."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\frac{1}{9}\"></span>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 3^{-2} = \\frac{1}{3^2} = \\frac{1}{9}\"></span>."},{"h":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"9\"></span>","why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3^2 = 9\"></span>, whereas the exponent here is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"-2\"></span>."}],"why":"Convert to exponential form: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"x = 3^{-2} = \\frac{1}{9}\"></span>."}]],"id":"bridge-08","title":"Preview: Logarithms (Math II)","goal":"convert between exponential and logarithmic form and apply the basic logarithm rules","week":"8","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"logarithm","m":"the exponent needed to reach a number from a given base","tr":"logaritma"},{"t":"base","m":"the number $a$ being raised to a power in $\\log_a(x)$","tr":"taban"},{"t":"natural logarithm","m":"$\\ln(x) = \\log_e(x)$ where $e \\approx 2.718$","tr":"doğal logaritma"}]}},"bank":[],"exercises":[],"challenges":[],"cheat":null,"mocks":{},"extra":{}});
